## Statement

Let $X\in\mathbb R^{n\times p}$ have full column rank in a fixed-design
first-moment regression model. Suppose for some $\sigma^2>0$ the error vector
$\epsilon_\beta:=Y-X\beta$ has finite second moments,
$\mathbb E_\beta\epsilon_\beta=0$, and
$\operatorname{Cov}_\beta(\epsilon_\beta)=\sigma^2 I_n$ for every $\beta$,
where the vector covariance matrix has entries
$\operatorname{Cov}_\beta(\epsilon_{\beta,i},\epsilon_{\beta,j})$.
Then the unique least-squares estimator is
$$\widehat\beta=(X^TX)^{-1}X^TY,$$
it is unbiased for $\beta$, and its covariance matrix is
$$\operatorname{Cov}_\beta(\widehat\beta)=\sigma^2(X^TX)^{-1}.$$
No Gaussian assumption is made.

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** Integers $n,p\ge1$, a fixed-design first-moment regression model,
full-column-rank $X\in\mathbb R^{n\times p}$, and the finite second-moment
assumptions in the Statement.

[F1] The fixed design has response mean $\mathbb E_\beta Y=X\beta$.
[[def-fixed-design-linear-regression-model]].

[F2] Least-squares minimizers satisfy $X^T(Xb-Y)=0$; when $\ker X=\{0\}$ the
minimizer is unique. [[thm-least-squares-fitted-vector-and-solution-set]].

[F3] Rank-nullity gives
$\dim\mathbb R^p=\dim\ker X+\dim\operatorname{im}X$.
[[thm-rank-nullity]].

[F4] Expectation is linear on finite sums of integrable random variables.
[[cor-expectation-linearity-monotonicity-and-modulus-bound]].

[F5] Covariance is bilinear in finite linear combinations of square-integrable
random variables. [[thm-covariance-bilinearity-and-symmetry]].

[F6] Variance and covariance are expectations of centered products.
[[def-moments-variance-and-covariance]].

[A1] The error coordinates are square-integrable, have mean zero, and satisfy
$\operatorname{Cov}_\beta(\epsilon_\beta)=\sigma^2 I_n$ for $\sigma^2>0$.

## Proof

**Proof technique:** direct.

1.1 Full column rank means $\ker X=\{0\}$. By [F3], $\operatorname{rank}(X)=p$. For every nonzero $v\in\mathbb R^p$, $v^TX^TXv=\|Xv\|_2^2>0,$ so $X^TX$ is injective and hence invertible by rank-nullity. The normal equation in [F2] now gives the unique minimizer $\widehat\beta=(X^TX)^{-1}X^TY$. [F2, F3, algebra]

2.1 Write $A=(X^TX)^{-1}X^T$. By the fixed-design model [F1], $\epsilon_\beta=Y-X\beta$, so $Y=X\beta+\epsilon_\beta$. Step 1.1 gives $\widehat\beta=\beta+A\epsilon_\beta$. By [F4] and [A1], $\mathbb E_\beta\widehat\beta=\beta+A\mathbb E_\beta\epsilon_\beta=\beta.$ [F1, A1, F4, step 1.1, algebra]

3.1 By [F5] and [F6], the $(r,s)$ entry of the covariance matrix of $A\epsilon_\beta$ is $\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^n A_{ri}A_{sj}\operatorname{Cov}_\beta(\epsilon_{\beta,i},\epsilon_{\beta,j})=\sigma^2\sum_{i=1}^n A_{ri}A_{si}.$ Thus $\operatorname{Cov}_\beta(\widehat\beta)=\sigma^2AA^T$ by step 2.1. Since $X^TX$ is symmetric, $AA^T=(X^TX)^{-1}X^TX(X^TX)^{-1}=(X^TX)^{-1}.$ No step uses the shape of the error distribution beyond its stated moments. [A1, F5, F6, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra] ∎